{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"9276f6c74708ab2b","name":"card_event_uss_sullivans","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/Homefront/events/card_event_uss_sullivans","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"USS THE SULLIVANS","text":"Target unit and the top unit of your deck each get +1+1. Draw a card.","flavorText":"Commissioned in September 1943, USS The Sullivans (DD-537) was a Fletcher-class destroyer named in honor of the five Sullivan brothers who perished aboard the USS Juneau in 1942. It was the first US Navy ship named after more than one person. Assigned to the Pacific Fleet, The Sullivans participated in numerous key campaigns during WWII. She screened aircraft carriers in major operations including the Marshall Islands, the Battle of the Philippine Sea, and the Marianas campaign. In October 1944, she played a defensive role during the Battle of Leyte Gulf, engaging enemy aircraft and rescuing downed pilots. Later, during operations off Okinawa, the ship faced intense kamikaze attacks. On 27 April 1945, a kamikaze narrowly missed, crashing into the sea close aboard. Despite the hazards, she continued escort and rescue duties with distinction. Throughout her wartime service, The Sullivans rescued over 100 aviators and endured numerous air raids without serious damage. She earned nine battle stars for her actions in the Pacific. After the war, she served in various capacities before being decommissioned in 1965. Her legacy as a tribute to familial sacrifice and naval service continues today as a museum ship in Buffalo, New York.","faction":"USA","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["subtype.navy"],"Name":"card_event_uss_sullivans","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_uss_sullivans_title","SourceString":"USS THE SULLIVANS","LocalizedString":"USS THE SULLIVANS"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_uss_sullivans_text","SourceString":"Target unit and the top unit of your deck each get +1+1. Draw a card.","LocalizedString":"Target unit and the top unit of your deck each get +1+1. Draw a card."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_uss_sullivans_history","SourceString":"Commissioned in September 1943, USS The Sullivans (DD-537) was a Fletcher-class destroyer named in honor of the five Sullivan brothers who perished aboard the USS Juneau in 1942. It was the first US Navy ship named after more than one person. Assigned to the Pacific Fleet, The Sullivans participated in numerous key campaigns during WWII. She screened aircraft carriers in major operations including the Marshall Islands, the Battle of the Philippine Sea, and the Marianas campaign. In October 1944, she played a defensive role during the Battle of Leyte Gulf, engaging enemy aircraft and rescuing downed pilots. Later, during operations off Okinawa, the ship faced intense kamikaze attacks. On 27 April 1945, a kamikaze narrowly missed, crashing into the sea close aboard. Despite the hazards, she continued escort and rescue duties with distinction. Throughout her wartime service, The Sullivans rescued over 100 aviators and endured numerous air raids without serious damage. She earned nine battle stars for her actions in the Pacific. After the war, she served in various capacities before being decommissioned in 1965. Her legacy as a tribute to familial sacrifice and naval service continues today as a museum ship in Buffalo, New York.","LocalizedString":"Commissioned in September 1943, USS The Sullivans (DD-537) was a Fletcher-class destroyer named in honor of the five Sullivan brothers who perished aboard the USS Juneau in 1942. It was the first US Navy ship named after more than one person. Assigned to the Pacific Fleet, The Sullivans participated in numerous key campaigns during WWII. She screened aircraft carriers in major operations including the Marshall Islands, the Battle of the Philippine Sea, and the Marianas campaign. In October 1944, she played a defensive role during the Battle of Leyte Gulf, engaging enemy aircraft and rescuing downed pilots. Later, during operations off Okinawa, the ship faced intense kamikaze attacks. On 27 April 1945, a kamikaze narrowly missed, crashing into the sea close aboard. Despite the hazards, she continued escort and rescue duties with distinction. Throughout her wartime service, The Sullivans rescued over 100 aviators and endured numerous air raids without serious damage. She earned nine battle stars for her actions in the Pacific. After the war, she served in various capacities before being decommissioned in 1965. Her legacy as a tribute to familial sacrifice and naval service continues today as a museum ship in Buffalo, New York."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/USA/10_Homefront/T_uss_sullivans.T_uss_sullivans","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/USS_THE_SULLIVANS.USS_THE_SULLIVANS","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_uss_sullivans_photo","SourceString":"The Sullivans off Ponape Island, May 1944.","LocalizedString":"The Sullivans off Ponape Island, May 1944."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"isAttackBuff":true,"isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[{"name":"unitOnTop","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_uss_sullivans_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FS